[/asy]It is not difficult to show that $\triangle ADE \sim \triangle ABC$, so $\frac{DE}{BC} = \frac{AD}{AB}$.  On the other hand, $DE = 180^\circ - \angle BAC = 180^\circ - 90^\circ = 90^\circ$, so we have $BC = \frac{AB \cdot DE}{AD} = \frac{6 \cdot 90}{AD} = \frac{540}{AD}$. [/.tencent]We can also show that $\triangle AXD \sim \triangle ABC$, so $\frac{XD}{BC} = \frac{AD}{AB}$, which implies $XD = \frac{AD}{AB} \cdot BC = \frac{90}{AD} \cdot BC = 90$.[\answer]We now have the two equal circles.  Let $X'$ and $Y'$ be the points on the circle opposite to $X$ and $Y$, respectively.  Note that $X(c+a) = Y(c+a)$ so if $(c+a)$ were the radius of the circle (where $c = CD = CE$ was the normal distance from the circle center to $BC$) then we have $a = f(a)$, $b = g(b)$, and $f(9-a) = g(9-b)$ for $f(x) = \frac{x}{2}$ and $g(x) = \frac{9-x}{2}$. The solution set is $(6, 6)$ and therefore $c + a = 6$, so the radius of our circle is $3$, and AED has area $\frac{1}{2} \cdot 6 \cdot \frac{90}{6} = 45$.  Therefore, the area we seek is $9\pi - 45$. [/answer]
